// Dusty Miller: the Victorian built-wing salmon fly, in John Gray's simplified needle tube, a long-tailed shrimp-style fly.

id dusty-miller
name "Dusty Miller"
type salmon
imitates attractor
difficulty beginner
species salmon
description "A long tail of yellow and red bucktail over natural squirrel with pearl flash, a short fluorescent orange floss body, a sparse brown bucktail wing with orange flash, and orange and brown hackles in front."
history """
    One of the oldest standard salmon flies, Kelson called it (1895), crediting it to Jewhurst; J.
    H. Hale (1892) gives it as Mr Murdoch's. The name was in use by 1867: Herbert Maxwell sent
    Francis Francis a Dusty Miller for the Minnick in low, bright water, with an embossed silver
    body, a dark olive hackle and a mixed wing. Mary Orvis Marbury (1892) says it is sometimes
    called the Beryl. The dressings disagree on almost every part. Pryce-Tannatt (1914) counts it
    among the built-wing flies, with the Jock Scott and the Butcher: embossed silver tinsel over two thirds of the body,
    orange floss in front under a golden olive hackle, a speckled guinea fowl throat, and a wing
    of white-tipped black turkey under married strands of swan, bustard, florican and golden
    pheasant tail, with jungle cock cheeks, a topping and macaw horns. Kelson's has no body hackle and
    gallina in the wing; Hale's body is embossed silver all the way. This is John Gray's needle
    tube, the classic much simplified in the style of the long-tailed shrimp flies such as Ally
    Gowans's Cascade.
    """
fishing """
    Marbury's Dusty Miller is a large silver fly for later in the season, where the water is
    rough and rapid, or deep; Maxwell's was for low, bright water.
    """
sources {
    "Trout & Salmon Fishing: The Dusty Miller salmon needle tube fly (John Gray)" url="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/dusty-miller-fly.htm"
    "Internet Archive: The Salmon Fly (George M. Kelson, 1895)" url="https://archive.org/details/salmonflyhowtodr00kelsrich"
    "Internet Archive: How to Dress Salmon Flies (T. E. Pryce-Tannatt, 1914)" url="https://archive.org/details/howtodresssalmon00prycrich"
    "Internet Archive: How to Tie Salmon Flies (J. H. Hale, 1892)" url="https://archive.org/details/howtotiesalmonfl00hale"
    "Internet Archive: A Book on Angling (Francis Francis, 1867)" url="https://archive.org/details/cu31924003429226"
    "Internet Archive: Favorite Flies and Their Histories (Mary Orvis Marbury, 1892)" url="https://archive.org/details/favoritefliesthe00marb"
    "Fly Anglers Online: Just Old Flies, Dusty Miller (Eric Austin; archived)" url="http://web.archive.org/web/20240511172723/https://www.flyanglersonline.com/features/oldflies/part320.php"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/dusty-miller-10.jpg" page="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/dusty-miller-fly.htm" author="John Gray" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Gray's needle tube, side view"
}

// Gray: a stainless needle tube 15 mm long, 1.8 mm across. Assumption: a #12 treble behind it.
hook tube-needle length=15 treble=12
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    // Read from Gray's photo: the squirrel about 26 mm, the bucktail about 40 mm from where it is tied in.
    tail-lower squirrel-tail count=40 label="Natural squirrel tail"
    tail-yellow bucktail color=yellow count=40 label="Yellow bucktail"
    tail-red bucktail color=red count=40 label="Red bucktail"
    tail-flash krystal-flash color=pearl count=6 label="Pearl Krystal Flash"
    body silk-floss color=orange label="Fluorescent orange floss"
    wing bucktail color=brown count=25 label="Brown bucktail"
    flash krystal-flash color=orange count=5 label="Orange Krystal Flash"
    // Read from Gray's photo: the hackle fibres about 16 mm, the brown a little longer, swept well back.
    hackle-orange cock-hackle color=orange fibre=2.2 sweep=58 label="Orange cock hackle"
    hackle-brown cock-hackle color=brown fibre=2.6 sweep=58 label="Brown cock hackle"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.95
    wrap-thread to=0.67 note="A bed of thread about a third of the way along the tube."
    tie-in tail-lower length=3.6 angle=10 spread=40 side=under note="A sparse bunch."
    trim tail-lower
    tie-in tail-yellow length=5.6 angle=8 spread=40 note="Turn the vice through 180° first; a sparse bunch."
    trim tail-yellow
    tie-in tail-red length=5.6 angle=10 spread=40 note="A sparse bunch over the yellow."
    trim tail-red
    tie-in tail-flash length=5.8 angle=9 spread=30 note="A few strands, to taste."
    tie-in body
    wrap-thread to=0.82
    wind body to=0.82 turns=6 note="A short body, back to the tail and forward again."
    tie-off body
    tie-in wing length=5.2 angle=14 spread=40 note="Sparse."
    trim wing
    tie-in flash length=5.2 angle=14 spread=30 note="A few strands with the wing."
    tie-in hackle-orange
    wrap-thread to=0.88
    wind hackle-orange to=0.88 turns=3 note="Two or three turns."
    tie-off hackle-orange
    tie-in hackle-brown
    wrap-thread to=0.94
    wind hackle-brown to=0.94 turns=2 note="Two or three turns in front of the orange."
    tie-off hackle-brown
    whip-finish note="A neat head, two coats of Cellire No. 1 varnish."
}
